{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And they shall make an ark of shittim wood.</b> In all the other vessels it says 'and you shall make' — ועשית, 'and you shall make a cover' — ועשית כפרת, 'and you shall make a table' — ועשית שלחן, 'and you shall make a lampstand' — ועשית מנורת; but here it is written 'and they shall make' (ועשו). And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted this: Rabbi Yehudah bar Shalom said: the Holy One, blessed be He, said: let all come and be occupied with the ark, so that all may merit the Torah; and this 'occupation' is that they should all donate." "<b>And by way of</b> the plain sense (peshat): the ark was a kind of chest specially designated to store the tablets of the testimony within it, and because the Torah is first, therefore the ark preceded the other vessels. And the reason it is called ארון is from the root of אורה, because the Torah within it is called אור. As for the fact that it mentions 'and a cubit and a half its height' — the cubit of the Torah is six handbreadths, so the height of the ark comes to nine handbreadths, and the cover (kapporet), concerning which there is a tradition to our Rabbis of blessed memory that its thickness should be one handbreadth, by way of a gezerah shavah of 'פני פני,' would complete it to ten. And so we find the number ten alluded to also in the table, for there too Scripture mentions 'and a cubit and a half its height,' which would be nine handbreadths, and the frame (misgeret) of one handbreadth would complete it to ten. And likewise in the lampstand it is written (Exodus 25:31) ״תיעשה המנורה״ — with a yod; and therefore Solomon made ten lampstands — for it cannot be that the Torah should command one lampstand and Solomon come and make ten of them, and likewise ten tables and ten basins, and thereby transgress the prohibition of בל תוסיף. Rather, the Holy One, blessed be He, made known to Moses on the mountain that when they would build the Holy Temple they should make ten, and this was a tradition (kabbalah) in their hands, until the building of the Holy Temple arrived and Solomon made them as the tradition that was in their hands. And there is yet another allusion to the number ten in the lampstand: seven branches, plus the knob (kaptor), the cup (gevi'a), and the flower (perach) — behold, ten. And likewise in the Tabernacle it mentions ״ואת המשכן תעשה עשר יריעות״; also the height of the Tabernacle was ten cubits, like the height of the boards, as it says ״עשר אמות אורך הקרש״; also in the altar of burnt offering we find the number ten, for it is written ״חמש אמות אורך וחמש אמות רחב״ — behold, ten cubits that the altar was in length and breadth combined, and moreover its height was ten cubits, as I shall explain at the end of the parashah. Thus one learns that the number ten is inscribed in every vessel of the Tabernacle." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: there is in it an allusion and a testimony to the way of the Kabbalah — 'and they shall make an ark': the making of the ark was beloved like the Throne of Glory on high, as it says (Exodus 15:17): ״מכון לשבתך פעלת ה׳״ — teaching you that the Temple corresponds to the Throne of Glory. And the two cherubim, beloved as they are, correspond to heaven and earth, in which is the seat of the Holy One, blessed be He. And the entire Tabernacle was made only for the sake of the ark alone, for the Shekhinah dwelt within it, and all the miracles performed for Israel were performed through the ark, in which the Shekhinah dwelt. And it is written (Numbers 10:33): ״וארון ברית ה׳ נוסע לפניהם״, and it would kill the snakes and scorpions and slay all the enemies of Israel. Rabbi Elazar ben Pedat said in the name of Rabbi Yossi ben Zimra: two sparks would issue from between the two poles of the ark and kill the snakes and scorpions and burn the thorns, and the smoke would rise and billow, and the entire world would be fragrant from the scent, and the nations would say (Song of Songs 3:6): ״מי זאת עולה מן המדבר כתימרות עשן מקוטרת מור ולבונה מכל אבקת רוכל״ — thus far in the Midrash." "<b>And it may</b> further be explained: 'and they shall make an ark of shittim wood' — these are those who support those occupied with Torah; 'and you shall overlay it with pure gold' — because the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to make a shelter for both of them together, for those occupied with Torah and for those who support them, as it says (Ecclesiastes 7:12): ״כי בצל החכמה בצל הכסף״, and it is written (Proverbs 3:18): ״עץ חיים היא למחזיקים בה״ — it does not say 'to those who learn her' but 'to those who hold fast to her' — all the more so to those who learn her. 'And you shall insert the poles into the rings' — these are the multitudes who are obligated to sustain and support the learners of Torah, just as the poles sustained the ark; this is 'to bear the ark with them' — for since he is occupied with Torah, he is elevated on their account, as our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: ״גדלוהו משל אחיו״. And similar to this, our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: יבעון רחמי אתכליא על עליא — 'let the leaves pray for the grapes,' for were it not for the grapes, the leaves would not endure. And this parable teaches that the intent of planting the vine is only for the sake of the fruit, which is the cluster, and that is the essence, not the leaves; yet the leaves serve a great need to protect it from excess heat and cold and from the force of the winds and other accidents. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: all the cubits of the ark were broken; from here, that a Torah scholar should be humble and his heart broken within him." "<b>And by way of</b> reason (sekhel): 'and they shall make an ark' — it is possible to interpret the matter of the ark, which held the tablets of the covenant, and the cover that was upon it with its two cherubim, as alluding to three things that are in man: the body, the soul, and the intellect, and it alludes to the ultimate of good recompense and the immense reward that is theirs through the knowledge of Torah. For the ark was made of shittim wood from the desert, an allusion to the body of man; and the four rings on the ark correspond to the four elements in the body of man, which sustain and bear it; and the two poles in the rings correspond to the two cords in the body of man from head to foot, namely the spine and one cord close to its side; the two tablets of the covenant within it correspond to two special organs in the body of man through which one receives the wisdom of Torah, and they are the brain and the heart; and the two cherubim allude to the soul and the intellect, one male and one female, for the soul and the intellect are like male and female in imagination — one acts and the other receives." "<b>Now you already</b> know that the male is the root, and this is the saying of Shelomo, peace be upon him (Proverbs 19:2): ״גם בלא דעת נפש לא טוב״ — 'also without knowledge the soul is not good'; he explained that there is no perfection for the soul without the intellect, and it is unnecessary to speak of formation in the lower world, the nether world, for even in the middle world, which is the world of the spheres, there is male and female — they are the sun and the moon that are in the middle world. Therefore they were likened in the Torah, in the dream of Yosef, to the male and female among the lower beings. Also the sun and the moon that are in the middle world are an example of the male and female which are in the supernal world, that is, active and receptive. And the cheruvim were 'of beaten work' (מקשה), because the intent is that the soul should return to become intellectual, divine, and both of them were taken and emanated from the gold of the kapporet itself. The kapporet alludes to the Throne of Glory, for there is the root of wisdom, and kapporet is from the language of atonement (כפרה), and its return to its root is not possible without atonement and without knowledge of the Torah, which restores the soul to its root. And what emerges for you from this is that with knowledge of the Torah that is in the Ark, the soul and the intellect, taken and emanated from the Throne of Glory, ascend on high and return to their root. And from this Scripture says ״סוככים בכנפיהם על הכפורת״ — 'covering with their wings over the kapporet' — to allude that there is its root; and it says ״פורשי כנפים למעלה״ — 'spreading wings upward' — on account of their ascending on high, upward." "<b>And further,</b> it is possible to explain that the Ark alludes to the Garden, and the two tablets within it: one tablet has commandments of the intellect, and the second tablet has bodily commandments, corresponding to the Tree of Life and the Tree of Knowledge. The kapporet alluded to the 'aravot — that is, the uppermost firmament, the pure, which covers all the other firmaments. And the cheruvim upon the kapporet alluded to the angels above the aravot, from among whom prophecy goes forth to all the other prophets, and they were 'spreading wings upward' to receive influx from the Creator, blessed be He, whose prophecy to Moshe was without intermediary, and the speech came to him from between the two cheruvim." "<b>By way of the Kabbalah:</b> ״ועשו ארון״ — 'And they shall make an Ark.' The Ark is an exemplar of the Throne of Glory, and this is what it says: ״ואת תבנית כל כליו״ — 'and the pattern of all its vessels' — the Holy One, blessed be He, showed the pattern of all the vessels of the Mishkan. Therefore they would spread over the Ark a covering of techelet, for techelet resembles the firmament, and the firmament resembles the Throne of Glory, and so it is written in the parashah 'in the wilderness of Sinai' (Numbers 4:6): ״ופרשו בגד כליל תכלת מלמעלה״ — 'and they shall spread a garment of pure techelet above.' The expression ״כליל תכלת״ means that all of it resembled the techelet of above, in the likeness of the place of the Shekhinah above, which is entirely techelet. And because the Throne of Glory is composed of three elements in three appearances, as I have mentioned in connection with the verse (Exodus 24:10) ״כמעשה לבנת הספיר״ — 'like the work of sapphire brick' — therefore Betzalel needed to make three arks. And this is what our Rabbis of blessed memory drashed: three arks did Betzalel make — two of gold and one of wood; he set the wooden one inside a golden one, and the golden one inside a wooden one, and overlaid its edges with gold, as it says ״מבית ומחוץ תצפנו״ — 'you shall overlay it within and without' — thus it was overlaid with gold within and without, and they were three arks yet appeared as one ark. And therefore you will find in this parashah of the Ark every letter of aleph-bet except for the letter gimel, which the Torah wished to conceal here, for the concealment of the three elements of the Throne, which are three; and Betzalel alluded to this through the count of the arks he made. And corresponding to the three appearances in the Throne, you will find three appearances in the rainbow, and on this account the word ״קשת״ — 'rainbow' — is written three times in the parashah of the covenant of Noach." "<b>And you should be roused</b> to contemplate the wonders of the Ark: for just as the Throne of Glory — the living creatures bear it, yet it bears them, as our Rabbis of blessed memory drashed in Pirkei de-Rabbi Eliezer: ״והחיות נושאות את הכסא״ — 'and the living creatures bear the Throne' — the meaning of 'bear' is that they appear as though they bear, yet they are borne; and from this the liturgical poet said: ״חיות נראות כנושאות״ — 'the living creatures appear as bearers' — with the כ ('as') vocalized with a sheva. So too the Ark would bear the priests who carried it upon their shoulders, as our Rabbis of blessed memory drashed in tractate Sotah: when Israel crossed the Jordan, the Ark bore its bearers and crossed, as it says (Joshua 4:11): ״ויהי כאשר תמו כל העם לעבור ויעבור ארון ה׳ והכהנים לפני העם״ — 'and it was, when all the people had finished crossing, that the Ark of YHWH crossed, and the priests, before the people' — and it is written (ibid.): ״נתקו כפות רגלי הכהנים אל החרבה וישובו מי הירדן למקומם״ — 'the soles of the priests' feet were drawn up to the dry ground, and the waters of the Jordan returned to their place' — the meaning of 'were drawn up' is backwards, toward the bank where they had been standing, and immediately the waters of the Jordan returned to their place. If so, how did the Ark cross? Rather, conclude from this: the Ark bore its bearers and crossed the Jordan — this is the sense of ״ויעבור ארון ה׳ והכהנים״ — 'the Ark of YHWH crossed, and the priests' — using the singular ויעבור, not ויעברו. And just as the Throne is borne by four living creatures, so the Ark was borne by four men, as you find in the chapter 'Shetei Ha-Lechem': when they carry the Ark upon their shoulders, face to face with their backs outward, and its staves lie along the width of the Ark, and four would carry it — two from this side and two from that side; end of quotation. And just as sparks of fire would go forth from between the two staves of the Ark, as they mentioned in the Midrash, so too from the flashes of the Throne of Glory, the pure and awesome, the existence of the chashmal is found, from which flows the influx of the holy life-force, and the chashmal and the living creatures surround the Throne. And regarding the matter of the cheruvim I shall reveal to you a little of the secret of the chashmal, and that the Ark is called by the name ארון on account of אורה (light), and on account of its encompassing the great power called נורא (awesome), which is hidden within it, in the manner of what is written (Exodus 34:10): ״את מעשה ה׳ כי נורא הוא״ — 'the work of YHWH, for it is awesome.' And so we find that the Ark is called 'lord of all the earth,' as it says (Joshua 3:11): ״ארון הברית אדון כל הארץ״ — 'the Ark of the Covenant, lord of all the earth.' For the Shekhinah cleaves to the Torah, and you will find in Chronicles (II Chronicles 6:41): ״אתה וארון עזיך״ — 'You and the Ark of Your might' — spelled full with a yod. *) [Before us in all the editions it is deficient of the yod, according to its regular form.] And the reason it is called 'the Ark of the Testimony' is because it is a testimony to the Shekhinah, which dwells in Israel, in that the Ark was not subject to measure; and this is what our Rabbis of blessed memory drashed: 'the place of the Ark is not subject to measure' — the intent is to say that it took up no space at all, for it stood by miracle. And so the Glory — no one knows its place, and from this the angels say: ״ברוך כבוד ה׳ ממקומו״ — 'Blessed be the glory of YHWH from His place' — thus they drashed in Pesikta."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {2 "Regarding ״וארון ברית ה׳ נוסע לפניהם״, the midrashic elaboration that follows (killing snakes etc.) is a well-known rabbinic tradition expanding on this verse; the citation is given as Numbers 10:33.", 3 "The Aramaic phrase יבעון רחמי אתכליא על עליא is a known rabbinic saying (cf. BT Ta'anit 25b), here rendered in context; ״גדלוהו משל אחיו״ appears to be a partial quotation whose full referent is uncertain from this passage alone.", 8 "The asterisk note beginning 'לפנינו בכל הספרים' appears to be a printed marginal annotation (possibly by a later editor or typesetter) on the spelling of ארון עוזך in II Chronicles 6:41; rendered as part of the text as found, set off with the asterisk marker."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:04:56.409486757Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}